(c) Investigating Corruption 
Where any internal investigation into alleged non-compliance with ABC Laws and / or this Policy is commenced, 
it must be carried out confidentially with discretion and sensitivity by the persons who are properly authorized 
. 
Any internal investigation into non-compliance with ABC L aws and / or this Policy may include access to all 
personnel and business records and business premises. 
The commencement, progress and results of any internal investigation into non-compliance with ABC Laws and 
/ or this Policy should not be disclosed to or discussed with anyone other than those who have a legitimate need 
to know. This is important to avoid damage to the reputation of the individuals involved who may be cleared of 
any suspected wrongdoing. 

9. Red Flags 
All Employees of the Company should use their personal judgement to identify and make prompt reports of any 
Bribery and Corruption risks. 
The following list provides some examples of common identifiers of Bribery and Corruption risk factors or “red flags” 
in relation to third parties: 
• unusual payment/financial arrangements; 
• lack of visibility – or descriptions - of services provided; 
• unusually high commission payments; 
• lack of transparency of expenses and accounting records; 
• lack of qualifications or resources in relation to the services to be provided; 
• gifts or hospitality offered to Public Officials; 
• rumours or reports of Bribery and Corruption; 
• ownership links to Public Officials; 
• offers to exert influence through personal connections or intermediaries; 
• lack of a track record; 
• unclear ownership structure; 
• lack of permanent or substantial office or workplace premises; 
• requests for payments in cash; 
• request to hire a new staff related to a Public Official or any influential person with any  regulatory body, 
especially against unbudgeted staff positions; 
• late or urgent changes to payment arrangements; 
• payments made / Gifts and Hospitality taking place during a tender process;  
• reluctance to provide information and verification in response to due diligence requests;  
• refusal to acknowledge receipt of Code of Conduct or this Policy, or provide information on compliance with 
ABC Laws; and 
• requests to include/pay unsubstantiated disbursals in consultancy/agency payments 


The Company will make this P olicy available on its intranet portal for all Em ployees. An anti -Bribery and Corruption 
training program will be made available to all Employees, and completion thereof is mandatory as part of the induction 
process and annually by all existing employees . Training must demonstrate how employees can manage situations 
tactfully, including how to graciously decline inappropriate gifts or defer acceptance where approval is required, while  
addressing local social gift-giving norms for all countries where Dar Global has a presence. 
Each employee will be required to confirm that he/she has undertaken anti -Bribery and Corruption training. Failure to 
complete anti- Bribery and Corruption training may be considered a disciplinary offence that may lead to summary 
dismissal. 
